From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:46:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] rtk: a Codex hook in config.toml counts, and its sandbox
 writes RTK's history (#5711)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Codex reads its hooks from hooks.json and from the [hooks] tables of its
config.toml alike, so RTK's page said off while Codex ran the hook a user
had written as [[hooks.PreToolUse]] there — and switching RTK on would
have run rtk's installer to add a second hook of the same command to
hooks.json, with RTK.md and AGENTS.md to match. has reads both sources
now, so the row says on and switching it on is no-op; switching it off
says config.toml's hook is the user's to take out, instead of pretending
done or leaving a hook behind. The read follows edit.TOMLWithout(raw,
"projects") so a 1.5 MB config.toml costs a decode of the hooks tables
alone, as #2278 left the file's other readers.

A matcher group's nested hooks array counts too ([[hooks.PreToolUse.hooks]],
command and its Windows-only commandWindows form): Codex trusted
laweit-lxy's hook of that shape in its hooks.state
(config.toml:pre_tool_use:0:0) while the flat-only read said off.

And in Codex's elevated Windows sandbox its commands run as Codex's own
sandbox account, which reads rtk's history folder (%LOCALAPPDATA%\rtk)
but can't write it (only an inherited (RX)), so rtk's hook there rewrote
the command, ran it, and silently recorded nothing — the count on the
page never moved. Codex makes every folder of [sandbox_workspace_write]
writable_roots writable for its sandbox accounts when it starts, so
switching RTK on for Codex adds that folder there (best effort: the
page's sandbox note still says why nothing is counted when the write
fails). An unelevated sandbox wasn't tried with this, and gets no write.
The sandbox note (elevated) now says the way out, and is gone once the
folder is writable, as nothing is left to explain. laweit-lxy confirmed
the write working on a real Windows box: icacls shows the explicit
CodexSandboxUsers:(OI)(CI)(M), and a fresh codex exec session's ls -la
was rewritten to rtk ls -la and landed the first Codex-sandbox row in
history.db.

Windows itself wasn't at hand (darwin): the config.toml shapes run as
fixture files against the same parsers the page reads, and the write
runs against a fake home's config.toml with the Windows-only gate made
a var; a real Windows 11 ARM64 Codex with an elevated sandbox is
NOT-RUNNABLE here.

Ablation: with has back to hooks.json alone, TestRTKCodexTomlHooks
fails at "yet Codex showed off"; with the nested hooks array unread,
TestRTKCodexTomlNestedHook fails at the same words; with the
writable_roots write out, TestRTKCodexGainWritable fails at
"writable_roots = [], want rtk's folder alone". All green again with
the change back.

Run at upstream/main 7e5cebb86 + this commit, macOS 15 (darwin/arm64):
internal/library whole ok.

